# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
#
# gpon/Kconfig -- the hardware-decoupled GPON protocol core, common to every
# OpenWrt GPON target in this tree.
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   The Kconfig half of the build wiring for drivers/net/gpon/: the one symbol
#   a vendor GPON driver selects to pull in the shared PLOAM / OMCI / GEM
#   protocol core.  It declares no driver of its own -- nothing here probes a
#   device, and there is no hardware called "GPON core".
#
# WHY IT IS COMMON -- and which targets and architectures compile it
#   Operator, 2026-08-05: "en openwrt deberia estar estructurado algo asi:
#   rtl960x* para la familia para tener codigo comun" ... "la idea es poner en
#   comun el codigo que corresponde para no tener mucho duplicado", and on the
#   two per-target monoliths that each carried a private copy of the protocol:
#   "mal, poner en comun".
#   G.984/G.988 are specifications, not chip facts, so exactly ONE copy of them
#   may exist in the tree.  This file is authored once, in the shared files-
#   tree target/linux/gpon-common/files-6.18/, and reaches both targets through
#   FILES_DIR (see their target Makefiles).  Compiled by:
#     - realtek-elnath  RTL9607F (Cortina)  aarch64, LITTLE-endian
#     - realtek-luna    RTL960xC (Luna)     MIPS32,  BIG-endian
#     - dev/rtl9607c-test on x86-64 through fuzz_shims/, where the same sources
#       are fuzzed under ASan+UBSan without a board
#   The prefix is gpon_ and not luna_ on purpose: the layer must also serve
#   the future ARM OLT and other brands, so a Realtek-named prefix would be too
#   narrow for what it covers.
#
# THE CORE/SHELL RULE IT OBEYS
#   Everything built from drivers/net/gpon/ is FUNCTIONAL CORE: it decides, it
#   never does.  It parses wire bytes, runs the state machines and builds reply
#   buffers; the per-target shell (drivers/net/ethernet/{realtek,cortina}/)
#   reads and writes registers, arms DMA, services interrupts and calls the
#   core through an op table.
#   ==> NO OBJECT WIRED HERE MAY EVER GAIN AN MMIO ACCESS.  No readl/writel, no
#   ioremap, no device pointer, no sleeping, no kernel timers -- time is an
#   explicit input.  A file that needs any of those belongs in the target's own
#   files- tree, not in this directory.  gpon_core_purity_test in
#   dev/rtl9607c-test/ is the re-runnable proof, not this comment.
#
# NOT A PATCH, BY HARD RULE
#   Driver wiring lives in a browsable source file under files-<ver>/, never in
#   a patches-<ver>/*.patch -- not even for Kconfig or Makefile.  An AI-authored
#   patch is un-findable by the next human or LLM, and editing one keeps
#   silently re-breaking the source it applies to.

config GPON_CORE
	bool
	help
	  The hardware-decoupled GPON protocol core shared by every GPON target
	  in this tree: the PLOAM activation state machine, the ITU-T G.988 OMCI
	  responder and the GEM/T-CONT provisioning lifecycle.

	  There is nothing to choose here.  A vendor GPON driver that wants the
	  shared core selects this symbol; no target's config-<ver> mentions it,
	  so the "enabled it for one target and forgot the other" failure mode
	  cannot occur.

	  It is deliberately a bool and not a tristate: both GPON drivers in this
	  tree ship built in (CONFIG_CORTINA_GPON=y, CONFIG_LUNA_GPON=y), an
	  ONU has <=64 MB RAM and a small NAND, and a separate gpon_core.ko would
	  cost module-loader overhead and a MODULE_LICENSE translation unit while
	  buying nothing.  Built in also means the core's entry points are plain
	  globals -- correct as long as every consumer is built in too.  A vendor
	  driver set to =m would fail modpost with "undefined!" on those symbols;
	  the fix then is EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL in the core, not a change here.

config OLT_ZTE
	bool "Serve ZTE OLTs"
	depends on GPON_CORE
	default y
	help
	  What only a ZTE OLT needs of the ONU: the "zte" far-end profile
	  (OMCC 0xa2, ME 138 and ME 340 on the VEIP), ZTE's vendor MEs 247
	  (extended ONU-G) and 65294 (SNTP), and PLOAM 0x81 (ZTE's
	  Disable_Serial_Number).  Off, a ZTE OLT's messages are unknown to
	  this image: they land in /proc/gpon_unknown and /proc/ploam_unknown,
	  and a board declaring ME 247 or 65294 is refused at boot.

	  Operator, 2026-10-18: each OLT vendor's support is one
	  CONFIG_OLT_<VENDOR>, so a small ONU carries only the OLTs it meets.

	  Cost (diag_cost.py --whole-image OLT_ZTE, 2026-10-18): 896 B of
	  vmlinux on the X100DG (MIPS); 4096 B on the X400AXF (aarch64),
	  exactly one page, so there the alignment quantum is what shows.

config GPON_FLOW_OFFLOAD
	bool
	depends on GPON_CORE
	help
	  The TC hardware-offload lifecycle, shared by every family whose
	  silicon has a flow accelerator: reading a TC rule into a 5-tuple,
	  keeping the cookie -> flow map, refusing a cookie the other direction
	  already installed, tearing entries down, answering a stats poll.  A
	  family supplies an engine (struct gpon_flow_ops) and nothing else.

	  ⚠ SELECT IT ONLY ON A TARGET THAT HAS AN ENGINE.  It is not a
	  no-op when unselected -- it is ABSENT, which is the point: a board
	  with no accelerator carries none of these bytes, and this tree ships
	  to parts with <= 64 MB of RAM and a small NAND.

	  Selected by each family's accelerator backend. Targets without an
	  accelerator must not select this shared lifecycle.

config GPON_FLOW_DIAG
	bool "GPON accelerator diagnostics (why a flow was NOT offloaded)"
	depends on GPON_FLOW_OFFLOAD
	default y
	help
	  The fourth member of the GPON_<SUBSYSTEM>_DIAG family, and the first
	  that is not about the protocol: it answers "the accelerator is on and
	  the traffic is still on the CPU -- why".

	  The lifecycle in drivers/net/gpon/ refuses a TC rule at SIXTEEN
	  distinct points and reports them through four errnos of its own plus two
	  it passes through, of which -EOPNOTSUPP alone carries ELEVEN.  So a
	  family counting refusals by errno cannot tell "the rule was not IPv4"
	  from "the engine's table is full" from "the WAN carries a VLAN tag no
	  engine here can push".  This names the
	  cause where it is actually decided -- in the core -- and both families
	  render the SAME line, so one reader serves the whole fleet.

	  It replaces two private spellings of the same idea: the Luna shell kept
	  a `refusals` count plus a `refuse_why` string and INFERRED that the
	  core had refused by watching whether its own counters moved during the
	  call; the Cortina shell mapped errno onto four buckets.  Neither could
	  name one of the eleven.

	  ★ IT DOES NOT TURN THE ACCELERATOR ON OR OFF.  That is
	  RTL9602C_FLOWOFFLOAD / LUNA_FLOWOFFLOAD / CORTINA_NI_FLOWOFFLOAD,
	  all default y. This symbol decides only whether the REPORTING is compiled
	  in -- and with it off the facility is ABSENT and says so, rather than
	  printing counters nobody measured.

	  ★ WHY default y: the same reasoning as its three siblings.  The
	  spy/dump capability is first-class and is not stripped for production;
	  the footprint rule (<= 64 MB RAM, small flash) is what this symbol
	  exists to honour.  Its cost in bytes is MEASURED per subtarget by
	  dev/ONU-test-case/diag_cost.py, never asserted.  The default is the
	  operator's to flip.

	  A board with no accelerator does not select GPON_FLOW_OFFLOAD and so
	  cannot reach this symbol at all: it carries zero bytes of it.
	  Table bind/unbind results also appear in dmesg as "flow-block", with
	  the netdev, command, binder and errno (rc=0 means accepted).

config GPON_GEM_DIAG
	bool "GPON data-path diagnostics (what the WAN path is waiting for)"
	depends on GPON_CORE
	default y
	help
	  The third member of the GPON_<SUBSYSTEM>_DIAG family, and it answers
	  the question every board in this project has asked in turn: the ONU
	  reaches O5, the OLT provisions it, and NO DATA GEM APPEARS.  Each time
	  that was diagnosed by hand-adding prints and reverting them.

	  It renders one line holding the two INPUTS and the two VERDICTS -- what
	  the OLT provisioned (ME 262 Alloc-ID, ME 268 GEM Port-ID, the live OMCC
	  binding), what is armed in silicon, the plan, and WHICH precondition is
	  missing.  That last field is the point: `WAIT` has FIVE causes and one
	  name, and telling "the OLT never authorised this Alloc-ID over PLOAM"
	  from "the OMCC transport is down" from "ME 268 never named a GEM port"
	  is the difference between three unrelated repairs.

	  MEASURED on the X111W 2026-09-13: the OLT completed a whole
	  provisioning burst, the ONU answered all 109 messages rc=0 including
	  the ME 262 Set assigning Alloc-ID 0x0100, and ~2 s later the OLT
	  DEACTIVATED it while /proc/gpon read `admitted=0 alloc_bound=0`.
	  Nothing in the driver could say which precondition was missing.

	  ★ The plan and the blocker both come from the PURE functions in
	  gpon_data_plan.h, so this line cannot disagree with the behaviour it
	  describes -- it reports, it never decides.

	  ★ WHY default y: same reasoning as its two siblings.  The spy/dump
	  capability is first-class and is not stripped for production; a build
	  that must shed every byte sets this to n and the facility is ABSENT,
	  not silenced.

config GPON_OLT_DIAG
	bool "GPON far-end capture (record the OLT's conversation for offline replay)"
	depends on GPON_CORE
	default y
	help
	  The fifth member of the GPON_<SUBSYSTEM>_DIAG family, and the subsystem
	  it reports on is the FAR END.  Every other member renders a line for a
	  human; this one records the exchange in a form that REPLAYS off the
	  board, because making an ONU work against an OLT nobody owns is a
	  dump+replay problem and a line in dmesg cannot be fed back into anything.

	  It holds a ring of (sequence, time, O-state, direction, bytes) records --
	  the SEQUENCE and the STATE, not just frames -- and it owns both the
	  renderer and the parser, so the host replays a capture through the same
	  code the board wrote it with.  Time is an EXPLICIT INPUT: this tier reads
	  no clock, so a replay is deterministic and a capture taken on one board
	  replays on another, or on x86 with no board at all.

	  The differential that consumes it is rtl9607c-test step 32: it feeds the
	  recorded downstream PDUs back through our own OMCI responder and compares
	  our answer with the recorded one.  A canned replay that echoes recorded
	  bytes proves nothing; that one can DISAGREE, and a disagreement is where
	  a foreign OLT's expectation differs from ours.

	  WHY THIS IS NOT A QUIRK TABLE.  Stock answers the same problem with a
	  detection-free bitmask: four masks in its flash MIB select up to 45 tiny
	  per-OLT plugins by filename.  Nothing in it detects an OLT -- a human
	  sets the mask per deployment.  We have ONE OLT on this bench, so a quirk
	  table built here would be shaped by one far end; the capture is what lets
	  a second one be analysed before anything is written.  See
	  dev/MEASURED-gpon_olt_diag.md.

	  ★ WHY default y: same reasoning as its four siblings -- the spy/dump
	  capability is first-class and is not stripped for production.
	  ⚠ AND IT IS THE ONLY MEMBER WITH A RAM COST, SO THE NUMBER IS MEASURED AND
	  STATED, NOT CHOSEN SILENTLY.  MEASURED 2026-09-15 from the built objects:
	  the ring symbol is 16400 B of BSS (GPON_OLT_RECORDS 256 * 64 B + a 16 B
	  header) in the one GPON driver an image builds, and the formatter is
	  3764 B of text on taroko / 3648 B on interaptiv -- the largest of the five
	  members, because it carries a PARSER as well as a renderer.  Beside the
	  ring, fixed tables the OLT's polling cannot evict (64 B a record): the
	  provisioning ledger (256 records), the last exchange of each kind the ONU
	  did not identify (64), and on Luna the PLOAM types it does not handle (32)
	  -- 20 KiB more on Cortina, 22 KiB on Luna; /proc/gpon_prov, /proc/gpon_unknown
	  and /proc/ploam_unknown.  A build that
	  must shed every byte sets this to n and the facility is ABSENT, not
	  silenced.  The default is the operator's to flip.

config GPON_OLT_DIAG_RECORDS
	int "Records per far-end capture ring (OMCI and PLOAM)"
	depends on GPON_OLT_DIAG
	range 16 4096
	default 256
	help
	  Each record is 64 B of BSS per ring (plus a 16 B header); Luna keeps two
	  rings (OMCI and PLOAM), Cortina one.  256 holds a lab provisioning; the
	  field X111W on our image needed 680 on 2026-10-02 (records=256
	  dropped=424), so a field build sets 1024 (build-openwrt.py variant
	  "field"): 65552 B per ring.  diag_cost.py states the cost per value.

config GPON_OMCI_DIAG
	bool "GPON OMCI per-message diagnostics (message type, ME, Get masks)"
	depends on GPON_CORE
	default y
	help
	  The ONE spelling of the per-PDU downstream OMCI trace line: message
	  type and name, AR/AK, ME class/instance, and for a Get the mask the OLT
	  asked for against the masks the responder answered, could not model and
	  could not fit.  `unsup != 0` is the defect that strands an OLT in a Get
	  audit loop, and no counter can show it -- only the per-message line can.

	  Every family emits the SAME line, so one reader serves the whole fleet.
	  A shell that cannot report the responder's answer prints `resp=n/a`,
	  never a zero mask: a mask nobody read must not render as a device
	  finding.

	  ★ WHY default y.  This tree's rule is that the protocol spy/dump
	  capability is first-class and is not stripped for production; the
	  footprint rule (<= 64 MB RAM, small flash) is what this symbol exists to
	  honour -- a build that must shed every byte sets it to n and the
	  facility is ABSENT, not silenced.  The SAMPLING policy stays with the
	  family (the Cortina shell keeps its `omci_trace` module parameter, the
	  Luna shells rate-limit the bulk types), so =y is not =chatty.  The
	  default is the operator's to flip.

config GPON_PLOAM_DIAG
	bool "GPON activation diagnostics (grant acceptance at Assign/Ranging/Deactivate)"
	depends on GPON_CORE
	default y
	help
	  One log line at each PLOAM activation transition -- Assign_ONU-ID,
	  Ranging_Time, Deactivate_ONU-ID -- carrying the family's BWmap
	  acceptance counters and the upstream/downstream ONU-ID readbacks.  It
	  is EVENT-driven: a handful of lines per activation attempt and none at
	  a settled O5, so it cannot flood.  A counter the silicon does not
	  establish prints as n/a, never as 0.

	  ★ WHY default y.  This tree's rule is that the protocol spy/dump
	  capability is first-class and is not stripped for production; the
	  footprint rule (<= 64 MB RAM, small flash) is what this symbol exists
	  to honour -- a build that must shed every byte sets it to n and the
	  facility is ABSENT, not silenced.  MEASURED 2026-09-06 (mipsel-BE,
	  -Os): see the size line recorded beside this symbol's introduction in
	  dev/ (the formatter object plus the Luna call sites).  The default is
	  the operator's to flip.
